The two compressed forms hxres emits, and the 68000 code that unpacks them.

aPLib
	An LZSS based lossless compression algorithm by Jorgen Ibsen.
	http://www.ibsensoftware.com

	The packer hxres writes is held byte for byte against apj.jar, the Java compressor
	Stephane Dallongeville wrote for rescomp, which SGDK carries as bin/apj.jar and
	documents in bin/apj.txt.

	The 68000 decruncher md.Unpack.aplib is written from vendor/SGDK/src/tools_a.s, whose
	header credits it as:
		by MML 2010
		Size optimized (164 bytes) by Franck "hitchhikr" Charlet
		More optimizations by r57shell

LZ4W
	A word oriented variant of LZ4 by Stephane Dallongeville, part of SGDK and documented in
	bin/lz4w.txt. LZ4 itself is Yann Collet's.

	The packer hxres writes is held byte for byte against lz4w.jar.

	The 68000 unpacker md.Unpack.lz4w has the structure of the one in
	vendor/SGDK/src/tools_a.s, whose header credits it as:
		by Stephane Dallongeville @2017
		decomp code tweaked by HpMan, optimized further by Malachi

	It is the same 256 entry dispatch table into the same fallthrough chains, because
	measurement says nothing else reaches that speed. It is not a transcription: the table
	and its chains are generated by five gas macros rather than written out, the long match
	base is held in a4 rather than reached PC relative, and the rare match from the
	compressed stream is a loop rather than a second unrolled chain and its table.

